[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I tested end-to-end reachability between the two affected PCs and found no network fault. PC1 (10.1.0.100) and PC2 (10.1.0.101) can currently communicate bidirectionally with 0% packet loss and sub-millisecond latency. Evidence: topology-wide reachability shows pc1 -> pc2 OK; targeted pings from pc1 to pc2 and from pc2 to pc1 both returned 4/4 replies (rtt ~0.7–1.2 ms). Interface state on both PCs is UP with correct addressing: pc1 eth0 at 10.1.0.100/24 and pc2 eth0 at 10.1.0.101/24, each with default via 10.1.0.1.

Mechanism: If an office access switch or a specific port were down, you would see link down and/or packet loss/timeout between the two hosts. Instead, ICMP round-trips are clean and consistent, which indicates Layer 2/Layer 3 forwarding across the access and distribution switches is healthy.

Localization: No anomalies observed on pc1, pc2, or their upstream switching path (aswa2/aswa3 to distribution). Both NICs report LOWER_UP and are passing traffic.

Root cause: No network-side fault is present at time of testing; the reported outage is not reproducible now.

Corrective fix: No network changes are required. If file sharing still fails, investigate host/application layer on PC2 (e.g., file-sharing service status and local firewall) and retry. If the issue was intermittent, capture the exact time of the next occurrence so we can correlate with interface logs, but present evidence shows the switch and ports are operating normally.
=== END ===